GCM: cellGcmGetLastFlipTime is the guest system time (us, sys_time_get_system_time's clock) of the last completed flip.
Toro's own patch (7xxx); candidate for upstream (the bug is upstream's, in v0.15.0 and v0.19.0).
What: a shared ps3_system_time_us() (sys_timer.c) is the guest system clock; sys_time_get_system_time returns it.
cellGcmSys stamps s_last_flip_time with it where a flip completes (the vblank ticker paths and SetPrepareFlip),
no longer with absolute host QPC nanoseconds at the flip request. Also corrects the NID comment on
cellGcmGetLastFlipTime (0x63387071; 0x21AC3697 is cellGcmAddressToOffset).
Why: the game's frame limiter func_0018F5B8 (called from the main loop at 0xB37E8) computes
sleep = period - (sys_time_get_system_time() - cellGcmGetLastFlipTime()), usleeps that (syscall 0x8D, lr 0x18F5E8),
then waits for its vblank semaphore (syscall 0x5C, lr 0x18F678). With unrelated clocks the limiter always slept its
whole period (29.4 ms) and the frame was period + all the frame's work: run v019c (v0.19, PS3_FTRACE 260-280 s)
41.4 ms per frame, the semaphore waits split between ~1-2 ms and ~16 ms (one vblank), 22.3 flips/s in the 3D scenes
(main: 29.1). With the shared clock but the stamp still at the request (run v019d) the game paced itself at exactly
its period, 29.38 ms p50 and 34.1 flips/s, faster than the VSYNC flips (SetFlipMode 2) the PS3 shows at 30.
The PS3 and RPCS3 stamp the last flip on the guest system clock when the flip is shown [src:RPCS3-GCM].

